    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-33662",
      "knowledge_point_id": "3ff1822c-afad-54f8-aa36-7e0de79d9d04",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Unit Tangent Vector at a Given Point",
      "problem": "What is the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=⟨t+1,\\,8\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,2{t}^{2}+3⟩$ at the point $P(2,0,5)?$",
      "worked_solution": "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(2,0,5)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2,0,5⟩ \\\\ ⟨t+1,\\,8\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,2{t}^{2}+3⟩ & =⟨2,0,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}t+1=2 \\\\ 8\\operatorname{ln}⁡t=0 \\\\ 2{t}^{2}+3=5.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(2,0,5)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,\\frac{8}{t},\\,4t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨1,\\,\\frac{8}{1},\\,4(1)⟩=⟨1,8,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{8}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+64+16} \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at $P(2,0,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(1)}{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}⟨1,8,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{9},\\frac{8}{9},\\frac{4}{9}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
